# A law is a program; a presentation is an enumerator. Here we check
# the law  succ ; double = double ; succ ; succ  on successor-generated
# samples: map both sides over the samples, compare wholesale.

def succ   = 1 ... ; +
def double = 2 _ ; *

def samples = (0 1 2 3 4 5 ; pack)
def lhs = [succ ; double]        ... ; map
def rhs = [double ; succ ; succ] ... ; map

samples >> lhs >> _ (samples >> rhs) >> eq? >> verdict >> print   # in1() = law holds

# ---- laws that need no samples at all --------------------------------
# The law above is TESTED: it holds at 0..5, and says nothing about 6.
# Some laws can be DECIDED instead. A program built from wiring
# (id/_/dup/drop/swap/pass), composition and juxtaposition, over words
# treated as uninterpreted, is a morphism of the free cartesian category
# on those words — and that category's word problem is solvable. So
# `sameCode` normalizes both sides and compares, which proves the law
# for every input and every interpretation at once.

def verdict2 = ("proved" | "not proved") ; merge

# the comonoid laws: copying is coassociative, cocommutative, counital
[dup ; _ dup] [dup ; dup _] ; sameCode ; verdict2 ; print   # proved
[dup ; swap]  [dup]         ; sameCode ; verdict2 ; print   # proved
[dup ; _ drop] [id]         ; sameCode ; verdict2 ; print   # proved

# the payoff: NATURALITY of copy, quantified over an arbitrary word.
# No amount of sampling can say this, because it ranges over programs.
[dup ; toStr toStr] [toStr ; dup] ; sameCode ; verdict2 ; print   # proved

# and it really discriminates — applying a word to one copy is not
# applying it to both
[toStr ; dup] [dup ; toStr _] ; sameCode ; verdict2 ; print       # not proved

# ---- the honest boundary ---------------------------------------------
# `double = 2 _ ; *` and `dup ; +` agree on every Int. They are NOT the
# same morphism of the free category, because `*` and `+` are only
# words here — some interpretation tells them apart. So:
#
#   proved      = equal under EVERY interpretation (a theorem)
#   not proved  = NOT a counterexample at any particular type
#
# A law that needs `+` to be commutative, or needs Int arithmetic, still
# belongs with the sampled laws above. Deciding those means normalizing
# modulo an equational theory (AC, ACU, a field) rather than a free one.
[double] [dup ; +] ; sameCode ; verdict2 ; print                  # not proved
